\section{Finding the links in a body}
\label{sec:course-links-recognition}

A body is an HTML string, and the links we care about are the values of
three attributes: [[href]], [[src]] and [[data-api-endpoint]].
Nothing else in the body needs to change.

\subsection{Why not an HTML parser}

The natural tool would be an HTML parser: read the body, walk the
elements, change the attributes, write it back.
We do not do that, for a reason that only shows in the files the user
keeps: a parser serialises the whole body again, in its own attribute
order, its own quoting, its own spelling of void elements and entities.
A one-link change would then be a diff of every line.
The bodies are diffed in git and compared by eye after every pull, so
every byte that did not need to change must not change.
A regular expression over the three attribute values leaves the rest of
the body untouched, and adds no dependency (the project has no HTML
parser today).

The limitations are the usual ones for a regular expression over HTML and
are acceptable here: an unquoted attribute value is not matched (the
editor always quotes), and a link written inside a [[<pre>]] block as an
example is rewritten too, which is usually what the author wanted.
<<constants>>=
LINK_ATTRIBUTE = re.compile(
  r'(?P<lead>\b(?:href|src|data-api-endpoint)\s*=\s*)'
  r'(?P<quote>["\'])(?P<url>.*?)(?P=quote)',
  re.IGNORECASE | re.DOTALL)
@

The one thing HTML does to an attribute value is entity-escape it: an
ampersand in a query string is stored as [[&amp;]], and pandoc writes it
that way too.
We unescape before looking at the URL, so that the URL functions below see
[[?wrap=1&verifier=x]] and not the entity, and escape the ampersand again
on the way out.
When the rewrite function declines (returns [[None]]) or returns the URL
it was given, the original text is kept verbatim: no unescape and
re-escape, no change of quotes.
That is what makes a body without course links come back byte for byte
the same.
<<functions>>=
def rewrite_link_attributes(body, rewrite):
  """Apply `rewrite(attribute, url)` to every link attribute in `body`.

  `rewrite` gets the lowercase attribute name (`href`, `src` or
  `data-api-endpoint`) and the unescaped URL, and returns the new URL
  or `None` to leave the link alone. Everything outside the three
  attribute values is returned unchanged.
  """
  def replace(match):
    url = html.unescape(match['url'])
    attribute = match['lead'].split('=')[0].strip().lower()
    new_url = rewrite(attribute, url)
    if new_url is None or new_url == url:
      return match.group(0)
    quote = match['quote']
    return f"{match['lead']}{quote}{new_url.replace('&', '&amp;')}{quote}"
  return LINK_ATTRIBUTE.sub(replace, body)
@

Let's verify the properties the rest of the chapter relies on: all three
attributes are visited with the entity undone, a declined rewrite leaves
the text byte for byte the same, and a changed URL is escaped again.
<<test functions>>=
def test_rewrite_visits_the_three_attributes_unescaped():
  seen = []
  def rewrite(attribute, url):
    seen.append((attribute, url))
    return None
  body = ('<a href="/a?x=1&amp;y=2" data-api-endpoint="/b">t</a>'
          "<img src='/c'>")
  assert rewrite_link_attributes(body, rewrite) == body
  assert seen == [('href', '/a?x=1&y=2'), ('data-api-endpoint', '/b'),
                  ('src', '/c')]

def test_rewrite_escapes_ampersands_and_keeps_quotes():
  body = "<a href='/old'>t</a>"
  out = rewrite_link_attributes(body, lambda a, u: "/new?a=1&b=2")
  assert out == "<a href='/new?a=1&amp;b=2'>t</a>"

def test_rewrite_leaves_other_attributes_alone():
  body = '<a title="/courses/1" href="/x">t</a>'
  assert rewrite_link_attributes(body, lambda a, u: "/y") == \
    '<a title="/courses/1" href="/y">t</a>'
@

\subsection{Recognising a course URL}

A link into a course is [[/courses/<id>]] followed by a path, optionally
preceded by the server's origin and by [[/api/v1]] (in the
[[data-api-endpoint]] attribute).
The query and fragment are kept apart from the path because the path
decides what the link points at while the query and fragment are carried
over unchanged.
<<constants>>=
COURSE_URL = re.compile(
  r'^(?P<origin>https?://[^/?#]+)?'
  r'(?P<api>/api/v1)?'
  r'/courses/(?P<course>\d+)'
  r'(?P<path>/[^?#]*)?'
  r'(?P<query>\?[^#]*)?'
  r'(?P<fragment>#.*)?$',
  re.IGNORECASE | re.DOTALL)
<<classes>>=
@dataclasses.dataclass
class CourseLink:
  """A link into a course, split into the parts we translate."""
  course_id: int
  path: str
  query: str = ""
  fragment: str = ""
  api: bool = False
@

An absolute URL is ours only if its host is the server we are talking
to; a link to another Canvas installation, or to any other site, is not a
course link however its path looks.
Without a server URL to compare against we therefore accept only relative
links.
Hosts compare case-insensitively, the scheme does not matter, and a
trailing slash on the path is dropped so that [[/courses/1/assignments/]]
and [[/courses/1/assignments]] are the same list.
<<functions>>=
def parse_course_url(url, server_url=None):
  """Split a course URL into a `CourseLink`, or return `None`.

  Absolute URLs are recognised only when their host is that of
  `server_url`; relative ones always.
  """
  match = COURSE_URL.match(url)
  if not match:
    return None
  if match['origin']:
    if server_url is None or not same_host(match['origin'], server_url):
      return None
  path = (match['path'] or "/").rstrip("/") or "/"
  return CourseLink(course_id=int(match['course']), path=path,
                    query=match['query'] or "",
                    fragment=match['fragment'] or "",
                    api=bool(match['api']))

def same_host(url_a, url_b):
  """Whether two URLs name the same host, ignoring case and scheme."""
  host_a = urllib.parse.urlsplit(url_a).netloc.lower()
  host_b = urllib.parse.urlsplit(url_b).netloc.lower()
  return host_a == host_b
@

<<test functions>>=
SERVER = "https://canvas.kth.se"

def test_relative_and_absolute_course_urls_are_recognised():
  relative = parse_course_url("/courses/59543/pages/x?a=1#top")
  absolute = parse_course_url(f"{SERVER}/courses/59543/pages/x?a=1#top",
                              SERVER)
  assert relative == absolute
  assert relative.course_id == 59543 and relative.path == "/pages/x"
  assert relative.query == "?a=1" and relative.fragment == "#top"

def test_api_endpoint_urls_are_recognised():
  link = parse_course_url(f"{SERVER}/api/v1/courses/59543/pages/x", SERVER)
  assert link.api and link.path == "/pages/x"

def test_other_hosts_and_other_sites_are_not_course_urls():
  assert parse_course_url("https://other.example.com/courses/1/pages/x",
                          SERVER) is None
  assert parse_course_url(f"{SERVER}/courses/1/pages/x") is None
  assert parse_course_url("https://youtube.com/watch?v=1", SERVER) is None
  assert parse_course_url("/files/1") is None

def test_course_root_and_trailing_slash():
  assert parse_course_url("/courses/1").path == "/"
  assert parse_course_url("/courses/1/").path == "/"
  assert parse_course_url("/courses/1/assignments/").path == "/assignments"
@

\subsection{Recognising a symbol}

A symbol is one of the four tokens followed by a path, with the same
query and fragment conventions.
Canvas percent-encodes the dollar signs in some exports
([[%24WIKI_REFERENCE%24]]), so both spellings are accepted.
<<constants>>=
SYMBOLIC_URL = re.compile(
  r'^(?P<token>(?:\$|%24)(?:CANVAS_COURSE_REFERENCE|WIKI_REFERENCE'
  r'|CANVAS_OBJECT_REFERENCE|IMS-CC-FILEBASE)(?:\$|%24))'
  r'(?P<path>/[^?#]*)?'
  r'(?P<query>\?[^#]*)?'
  r'(?P<fragment>#.*)?$',
  re.DOTALL)
<<classes>>=
@dataclasses.dataclass
class SymbolicLink:
  """A symbolic link, split into token, path, query and fragment."""
  token: str
  path: str
  query: str = ""
  fragment: str = ""
<<functions>>=
def parse_symbolic_url(url):
  """Split a symbolic URL into a `SymbolicLink`, or return `None`."""
  match = SYMBOLIC_URL.match(url)
  if not match:
    return None
  token = "$" + match['token'].strip("$").replace("%24", "") + "$"
  path = (match['path'] or "/").rstrip("/") or "/"
  return SymbolicLink(token=token, path=path,
                      query=match['query'] or "",
                      fragment=match['fragment'] or "")
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_symbols_are_recognised_in_both_spellings():
  plain = parse_symbolic_url("$WIKI_REFERENCE$/pages/x?a=1#f")
  encoded = parse_symbolic_url("%24WIKI_REFERENCE%24/pages/x?a=1#f")
  assert plain == encoded
  assert plain.token == WIKI_TOKEN and plain.path == "/pages/x"
  assert plain.query == "?a=1" and plain.fragment == "#f"

def test_course_urls_are_not_symbols_and_vice_versa():
  assert parse_symbolic_url("/courses/1/pages/x") is None
  assert parse_course_url("$CANVAS_COURSE_REFERENCE$/pages/x") is None
@

\subsection{The query string Canvas appends}

Canvas adds query parameters of its own to links: [[module_item_id]]
when a link was followed from a module, and [[verifier]], a per-file
access token, on file links.
Both are specific to the course the link was made in, so a symbol must
not carry them; every other parameter ([[wrap=1]] on a file link, say)
is part of what the author wrote and is kept.
Dropping a parameter means re-encoding the query, which may change the
spelling of the others (a [[%2F]] may become a [[/]]); the meaning is the
same.
<<constants>>=
VOLATILE_PARAMS = ("module_item_id", "verifier")
<<functions>>=
def drop_query_params(query, names):
  """Remove the parameters in `names` from the query string `query`.

  `query` starts with `?` or is empty; the result has the same form.
  """
  if not query:
    return ""
  kept = [(key, value)
          for key, value in urllib.parse.parse_qsl(query[1:],
                                                   keep_blank_values=True)
          if key not in names]
  return "?" + urllib.parse.urlencode(kept) if kept else ""
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_volatile_params_are_dropped_and_the_rest_kept():
  query = "?wrap=1&module_item_id=345&verifier=abc"
  assert drop_query_params(query, VOLATILE_PARAMS) == "?wrap=1"
  assert drop_query_params("?module_item_id=345", VOLATILE_PARAMS) == ""
  assert drop_query_params("", VOLATILE_PARAMS) == ""
@

\section{Looking things up in a course}
\label{sec:course-links-index}

Both directions need the same tables about a course, read in opposite
directions: on the way out an id must become a name, on the way in a name
must become an id.
We build the tables once per body in a [[CourseIndex]] and build each
table only when a link of its kind occurs.
A page body typically links to one or two other pages and nothing else,
and fetching the assignment list to translate it would be a request the
user pays for without getting anything.
The lists come from the [[canvasapi]] course object, which the cache
decorators in [[hacks/canvasapi.nw]] wrap for assignments, quizzes,
modules and module items, so on a warm cache most tables cost no
request at all; discussion topics, files and folders are not cached and
are fetched when first needed.
<<classes>>=
class CourseIndex:
  """Lazy name and id tables for the objects a course links to."""

  def __init__(self, course):
    self.course = course
    self._tables = {}
    self._files = {}
    self._folders = {}

  <<course index methods>>
@

\subsection{Objects named by their name}

Assignments, quizzes, discussion topics and modules are all looked up
the same way, so one table type serves them all; only the fetching call
and the name attribute differ.
Announcements are discussion topics that the plain listing leaves out,
so the topic table asks for both.
<<constants>>=
NAMED_KINDS = {
  "assignments": ("name", lambda course: course.get_assignments()),
  "quizzes": ("title", lambda course: course.get_quizzes()),
  "discussion_topics": ("title", lambda course:
    list(course.get_discussion_topics())
    + list(course.get_discussion_topics(only_announcements=True))),
  "modules": ("name", lambda course: course.get_modules()),
}
@

A name is not guaranteed unique---two assignments called
\enquote{Lab 1} are legal---so the name table maps a name to a list of
objects rather than to one object.
Callers that need one object decide what an empty or a long list means;
that decision is different when exporting (warn, the name is still the
best we have) and when importing (refuse to guess).
<<course index methods>>=
def table(self, kind):
  """Return `(by_id, by_name)` for the objects of `kind`."""
  if kind not in self._tables:
    name_attr, fetch = NAMED_KINDS[kind]
    objects = list(fetch(self.course))
    by_id = {obj.id: obj for obj in objects}
    by_name = {}
    for obj in objects:
      by_name.setdefault(getattr(obj, name_attr), []).append(obj)
    self._tables[kind] = (by_id, by_name)
  return self._tables[kind]

def name(self, kind, object_id):
  """Return the name of the object of `kind` with `object_id`, or None."""
  by_id, _ = self.table(kind)
  obj = by_id.get(object_id)
  return getattr(obj, NAMED_KINDS[kind][0]) if obj is not None else None

def is_ambiguous(self, kind, name):
  """Whether more than one object of `kind` has the name `name`."""
  return len(self.table(kind)[1].get(name, [])) > 1
@

When resolving a name we try it exactly first and fall back to a
case-insensitive match, because a title retyped by hand is the one
plausible source of a mismatch that is still safe to accept.
We do not go further than that: no substring, no regular expression.
The names in a symbol were written by this tool from the real names, and
a fuzzy match that quietly picks the wrong assignment is precisely the
corruption the translation exists to prevent.
Contrast [[match_item_by_regex]] in [[content.nw]], which serves names a
human typed into a YAML header and where a regular expression is the
convenience the user asked for.
<<course index methods>>=
def candidates(self, kind, name):
  """Return the objects of `kind` named `name`, exactly or ignoring case."""
  _, by_name = self.table(kind)
  if name in by_name:
    return by_name[name]
  folded = name.casefold()
  return [obj for candidate, objects in by_name.items()
          if candidate.casefold() == folded
          for obj in objects]
@

\subsection{Module items}

A module item is named by its module and its title, so its table is the
module table with every module's items fetched.
Building it costs one request per module, which is why it is a table of
its own that only a link to a module item pays for.
<<course index methods>>=
def module_items(self):
  """Return `{item id: (module, item)}` for every item in the course."""
  if "module_items" not in self._tables:
    by_id = {}
    for module in self.table("modules")[0].values():
      for item in module.get_module_items():
        by_id[item.id] = (module, item)
    self._tables["module_items"] = by_id
  return self._tables["module_items"]

def module_item_candidates(self, module_name, title):
  """Return the items titled `title` in the modules named `module_name`."""
  return [item
          for module in self.candidates("modules", module_name)
          for item in module.get_module_items()
          if item.title == title]
@

\subsection{Pages}

Pages are the easy case on the way out (the slug is in the URL) and the
subtle case on the way in.
Canvas derives the slug from the title, and keeps a page reachable under
its old slugs after a rename, so asking Canvas for the slug directly is
both the cheapest and the most faithful lookup: it follows the same
redirects the browser would.
Only when Canvas has no page under the slug do we fall back to a page
whose title produces the slug, which catches a page that was recreated
by hand in the new course under the same title.
The slug derivation is Canvas's: lower case, and every run of characters
that is not a letter, digit or plus sign becomes a single dash.
<<course index methods>>=
def page_candidates(self, slug):
  """Return the pages reachable under `slug`, by redirect or by title."""
  try:
    return [self.course.get_page(slug)]
  except Exception:
    pass
  return [page for page in self.course.get_pages()
          if page.url == slug or slugify(page.title) == slug]
<<functions>>=
def slugify(title):
  """Return the URL slug Canvas derives from the page title `title`."""
  return re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9+]+", "-", title.lower()).strip("-")
@

\subsection{Files}

A file is named by its path below the course files root, the way a
Canvas export names it.
On the way out we have a file id and need its folder: one request for
the file, one for the folder, both remembered so that a body with many
links to the same folder pays once.
Canvas's root folder is called [[course files]] and every folder's
[[full_name]] starts with it, so that prefix is stripped from the path
and put back when resolving.
<<constants>>=
ROOT_FOLDER = "course files"
<<course index methods>>=
def file_path(self, file_id):
  """Return `(folder path, display name)` for `file_id`, or None."""
  if file_id not in self._files:
    try:
      file = self.course.get_file(file_id)
      folder = self.folder(file.folder_id)
      self._files[file_id] = (folder_path(folder), file.display_name)
    except Exception:
      self._files[file_id] = None
  return self._files[file_id]

def folder(self, folder_id):
  """Return the folder with `folder_id`, fetching it once."""
  if folder_id not in self._folders:
    self._folders[folder_id] = self.course.get_folder(folder_id)
  return self._folders[folder_id]
<<functions>>=
def folder_path(folder):
  """Return the path of `folder` below the course files root."""
  path = folder.full_name
  if path == ROOT_FOLDER:
    return ""
  if path.startswith(ROOT_FOLDER + "/"):
    return path[len(ROOT_FOLDER) + 1:]
  return path
@

On the way in we have the path and need the file id.
[[canvasapi]] resolves a folder path to the list of folders along it,
ending with the folder asked for, and the folder lists its files.
A file has two names: [[display_name]] is what the user sees and what
the export wrote, [[filename]] is the name on disk, which Canvas may
have altered (a space becomes a plus).
The display name is matched first, the filename as a fallback.
<<course index methods>>=
def file_candidates(self, folder, name):
  """Return the files named `name` in the folder at path `folder`."""
  try:
    folders = list(self.course.resolve_path(folder) if folder
                   else self.course.resolve_path())
    files = list(folders[-1].get_files())
  except Exception:
    return []
  return ([file for file in files if file.display_name == name]
          or [file for file in files if file.filename == name])
@

\subsection{A fake course to look links up in}
\label{sec:course-links-fake}

The tests need a course with one object of each linkable kind, and a
second course with the same names but other ids to stand in for next
year's instance.
The numeric ids are parameters so that the twin is the same function
called twice.
Two assignments share a name to exercise ambiguity, and the file's
display name contains a space and differs from its filename, as it does
in Canvas.
<<test functions>>=
def obj(**attrs):
  return types.SimpleNamespace(**attrs)

IDS = {"assignment": 123456, "lab": 7, "lab2": 8, "quiz": 34,
       "topic": 56, "announcement": 57, "module": 12, "item": 345,
